  FINDING           the two agree to within a factor of 1.70, so the correction is the
                    clock pair and not something this run invented. They are not owed an exact
                    match: A7 compared crystals directly and this compares the source's audio
                    clock against this Mac's monotonic clock through a radio and a jitter buffer.

pair cadence, and it is not the link
  the 5 ms row has 50.76 per cent of the population late, in clusters of one
  frame separated by gaps of one frame. That alternation is not something a radio can do:
  a burst is consecutive by definition, so no link leaves every second frame on time. Two
  Opus frames ride in one captured packet, so both arrive at one instant while the second
  sits a frame later in stream time and its excess is exactly a frame lower. A6.1 measured
  the same thing from the other side at -4.996 ms per pair at p50, 96 per cent of pairs
  inside the [-5,-4) ms bucket over 8998, 9000 and 120004 pairs, and found the first member
  is the one that goes late in practice: 524 against 384, 476 against 354, 8594 against 6391.

  What that establishes is a FLOOR: a target below the pair spacing cannot hold both members
  of a pair, so 5 ms is structurally unreachable on this sender for a reason that has
  nothing to do with the air. What it does not establish is that spacing the pair in the
  sender would be an improvement: that would also delay the second frame by a frame in real
  time, and whether the floor it removes is worth the delay it adds is arithmetic nobody has
  done. An argument from this structure for spacing was made and retracted earlier in this
  session for being wrong by a sign; nothing here revives it.

