  * the shape above 20 ms says whether this is one heavy distribution or a normal
    regime with a second class of stall behind it. A figure at 30, 50 or 80 ms
    authorises nothing: what the latency budget pays is decided elsewhere.
  a rate is a dash where the run saw fewer clusters than one needs. That is not a
    rate of zero, it is a count too small for a rate, and the count is beside it.
  the last column is the per-block spread of the cluster rate. Uncertainty here
    comes from time blocks and never from a binomial over frames: the frames inside
    a cluster are one event, so a binomial would overstate the precision by the
    mean cluster size.

